{
  "schema": 1,
  "note": "M32 (D-069): the keyboard map. The runtime's keyboard driver (external/rexglue-sdk/src/input/mnk/mnk_input_driver.cpp) turns keys into the Xbox 360 pad inputs that the game reads; every pad input has one keybind_* setting. This file is the one definition of what a person may bind: the pad inputs and what the game does with them (only behaviour seen in tests or reported by a person is named; the rest is hidden from the ordinary view and says so), the keys the runtime can see (names of external/rexglue-sdk/src/ui/keybinds.cpp that SDL produces, by their PC set-1 scan code: the runtime reads physical key positions, so the Settings window captures scan codes too), the keys that may not be bound and why, and the layouts. tools/r3hd_settings.py resolves it with a settings file's 'keyboard' section (a layout plus the inputs a person changed); tools/play.ps1 passes the result as keybind_* arguments; tools/m32_regression.py ties every name here to the SDK sources. Visual presets (Vanilla / HD+ / Custom) never change keys.",
  "default_layout": "modern",
  "slots": 2,
  "shared_keys": [{"key": "V", "inputs": ["left_shoulder", "right_shoulder"]}],
  "walk_magnitude": 0.5,
  "walk_note": "M49 (D-088): walk is a keyboard-only input. While its key is held the keyboard's left stick keeps its direction and gets this length (a fraction of full deflection; straight and diagonal alike). It is not a pad input, so it has no pad state of its own; yields = a layout's default key for it gives way to a key a person saved on another input before this input existed (a saved map stays valid). The magnitude is provisional: RE_NOTES M49 has the evidence.",
  "required": ["lstick_up", "lstick_down", "lstick_left", "lstick_right", "a", "b", "start"],
  "required_why": "menus are moved through with the left stick, confirmed with A and left with B; Start pauses and starts the game from its title screen",
  "fallbacks": {"Escape": "b", "Return": "start"},
  "fallbacks_why": "a key of this list that no input uses is added to its input, so Escape always goes back and Enter always presses Start unless the person gave those keys another job",
  "groups": [
    {"key": "movement", "name": "Movement"},
    {"key": "camera", "name": "Camera"},
    {"key": "actions", "name": "Actions"},
    {"key": "menus", "name": "Menus"},
    {"key": "other", "name": "Other controller buttons (not yet characterised)"}
  ],
